#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187994 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9609247-0e03-57ea-b5ae-e4d8317f21cd" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Contextual Problems Into Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "67d01a20-4e4b-5b03-a2a3-f4b21cd81662" :question/math-academy-id "e-10072" :question/problem "Since the year $2000$, a herd of antelopes multiplies at a rate proportional to their population. Also, the number of antelopes in the herd decreases by $20$ antelopes per year due to predators. What differential equation describes the rate of change in the population of antelopes at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time (in years) since the year $2000?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change in the number of antelopes per year increases in proportion to $P$ and decreases by $20.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t}=kP - 20,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a (positive) constant of proportionality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187995 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba211dba-23ad-5755-b1e3-082e13be59a2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Three-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "1b0483b6-a42d-51fb-9ccd-fe6cb2a7acc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10075" :question/problem "The volume of a sphere increases at a rate inversely proportional to its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process." :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a sphere **increases** at a rate **inversely proportional** to its **radius**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ *increases* at a rate *inversely proportional to its radius.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{k}{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{r} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{(\\frac{k}{r})}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{4\\pi {r}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK = \\dfrac{k}{4\\pi} > 0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{K}{r^3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187996 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "474f9c82-4ae7-58e0-9457-6bda9cab8973" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Coordinate Plane Problems Into Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "b657adbb-c3c4-54c2-8c2c-44ccc0bf9c3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10076" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative $y'(x)$ is **inversely** proportional to the square of the distance from $P$ to the origin. The derivative at the point $(-1,2)$ equals $2.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** $y'(x)$ is **inversely proportional** to the **square of the distance from $P$ to the origin.** *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *square of the distance from $P$ to the origin* is given by $x^2+y^2.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *inversely proportional to* the square of the distance from $P$ to the origin, we have \n\n$$\ny'(x) = k\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\ny'(x) = \\dfrac{k}{x^2+y^2}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that $y'(x) = 2$ when at the point $(-1, 2).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{k}{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{k}{5} \\\\ k & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\ny'(x)= \\dfrac{10}{x^2+y^2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188203 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d868c89-ca9e-589d-917e-cfec472a44f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Two-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "012abf23-a2df-5479-a38f-3c76c099a39f" :question/math-academy-id "e-524" :question/problem "The area of a circle decreases at a rate that's proportional to its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process." :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **radius**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional* to its *radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -kr\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi r^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-kr \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-kr \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{kr}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK = \\dfrac{k}{2\\pi} > 0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t} = -K.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d868c89-ca9e-589d-917e-cfec472a44f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Two-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce17c65-7d67-54af-bb8e-a4b548957fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500495} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc573f9-6cba-59f5-957c-6c9a34a77f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500566} {:answer/id #uuid "62f66835-664a-578e-9ff4-452e7799954b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=r+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500673} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1c7097-61f8-5853-b475-d4c6e09f84d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500728} {:answer/id #uuid "f60054c5-79dc-51b2-9d83-1ea1d762a52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f60054c5-79dc-51b2-9d83-1ea1d762a52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500992} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e2c35a2-00a1-5395-9ca6-d665e10ade6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8d1bea5-b961-5d22-9bfd-ba62f26db131" :question/math-academy-id "q-119161" :question/problem "The area of a circle increases at a rate that's proportional to the square of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **square of its radius**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *proportional* to the *square of its radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{kr}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9609247-0e03-57ea-b5ae-e4d8317f21cd" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Contextual Problems Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d780f55-423d-520a-ae84-39c36f6ee582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kt+4,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500689} {:answer/id #uuid "705637e0-e025-5947-a35a-621d180f650c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{t}+4,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500696} {:answer/id #uuid "a62277eb-8471-5a33-82c1-9e84c22b8e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-4kt,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500809} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e4717f-809c-579d-82ee-5d76825ca02e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kt+4,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500851} {:answer/id #uuid "be1dca02-392a-5ab7-8831-c08cee6fac63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kt-4,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d780f55-423d-520a-ae84-39c36f6ee582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kt+4,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500689} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b0ba67-775a-55e5-baad-f129063461aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d4170a5-9522-5ae2-b33d-b3d78a6d10b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-119185" :question/problem "An oil tank has a leak. The rate of change of the volume $V$ (in liters) of oil in the tank is directly proportional to the time $t$ (in minutes) passed since the tank started leaking. However, the tank is also refilled with $4$ liters of oil every minute. What is the differential equation that describes the rate of change of the volume of oil in the tank at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change in the oil volume decreases in proportion to time $t$ and increases by $4$ liters per minute. The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kt+4,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the (positive) constant of proportionality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9609247-0e03-57ea-b5ae-e4d8317f21cd" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Contextual Problems Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e7871ed-5cf7-50d7-886e-dbe5d153b9b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-kB-250,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373717} {:answer/id #uuid "e7dfbae4-5b1d-5477-8e3d-b60d9435539f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=kB-250t,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373718} {:answer/id #uuid "44700f85-a5f3-5ba3-b3be-7e5afb18cd1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=kB+250,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373719} {:answer/id #uuid "3a6edff6-d4af-5c48-a54d-c54c74640bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k{B}^{2}-250,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373720} {:answer/id #uuid "724c6b53-f1f0-58e4-8ecf-980b9958ff52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=kB-250,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "724c6b53-f1f0-58e4-8ecf-980b9958ff52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=kB-250,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373721} :answer-field/id #uuid "497d4119-43d8-54a4-9409-872704d2bb05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30342124-81c8-53c0-b466-7bdc97912fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-119189" :question/problem "A scientist experiments with bacteria. The number of bacteria $B(t)$ in his experiment increases at a rate proportional to the number of bacteria at that moment. Additionally, due to environmental conditions, $250$ bacteria die every hour.\nWhat is the differential equation that describes the rate of change in the population of bacteria at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time in hours?" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change in the number of bacteria per hour increases in proportion to $B$ and decreases by $250.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=kB-250,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a (positive) constant of proportionality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "474f9c82-4ae7-58e0-9457-6bda9cab8973" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Coordinate Plane Problems Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "769bd46c-ac71-5c9d-86aa-64d30f596ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500712} {:answer/id #uuid "83bbabc7-4044-5aff-aa2b-c2aefecaf00b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500740} {:answer/id #uuid "a89759bb-3bac-504e-b772-0676db77d102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500815} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9a3d00-b902-540f-adc1-1a154ca1cb1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500895} {:answer/id #uuid "e300ec87-21ad-50b7-8b42-3adbf0764a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83bbabc7-4044-5aff-aa2b-c2aefecaf00b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500740} :answer-field/id #uuid "03510cef-4190-5c10-95bf-9938ff3a5ecb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94332050-ff41-5fa8-9cce-c854bd7e0d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143107" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the squared distance from $P$ to the origin. What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **squared distance from $P$ to the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *squared distance from $P(x,y)$ to the origin* is given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the squared distance from $P$ to the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot ({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "474f9c82-4ae7-58e0-9457-6bda9cab8973" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Coordinate Plane Problems Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bef70d9-620b-5bc2-9ce0-41b67d6e9c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500586} {:answer/id #uuid "83cd57a1-e672-5e0d-b765-9410b2bf93a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500741} {:answer/id #uuid "d0606410-77b4-5ccd-9d71-85600bd1e077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500915} {:answer/id #uuid "f46f16a9-9135-5dc7-811d-9d40d9a99198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k|y|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500986} {:answer/id #uuid "fef5b82d-787d-5e00-8130-1277c4a51a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0606410-77b4-5ccd-9d71-85600bd1e077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500915} :answer-field/id #uuid "20aeab36-0a5e-5c4e-bb58-d247747394e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70f26979-0b45-521e-afad-0123af31ff5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143109" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis. What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $y$-axis * is given by $|x|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot |x|,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k|x|.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "474f9c82-4ae7-58e0-9457-6bda9cab8973" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Coordinate Plane Problems Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "566b270e-601c-556c-909e-228f098bd523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500642} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb54fc0-7b29-5b8d-9591-693a024ce49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500902} {:answer/id #uuid "d478dfbd-05e0-5dc2-bc83-3caf65a81571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=kxy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500919} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c89197-063d-5efb-987f-b5a6be72e4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500962} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd59d23-9e44-5a2b-9d57-caf1ff79fe38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffd59d23-9e44-5a2b-9d57-caf1ff79fe38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501013} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9cf447a-a943-5b06-b1a6-df34fd52eceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fc67f3e-fcb8-5740-a171-eb4e59b85b29" :question/math-academy-id "q-143110" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the square of the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis. What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **square of the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *square of the shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $x$-axis* is given by ${y}^{2}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot {y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k{y}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba211dba-23ad-5755-b1e3-082e13be59a2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Three-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49a327b9-75a5-53f6-8b43-cf618bcf3524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373801} {:answer/id #uuid "1df99bf7-929a-5903-abcf-591bb526f29e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373802} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbf909f-462f-5518-8013-a606f7cf41aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373803} {:answer/id #uuid "3d49d7ac-08ee-59ab-9b44-461517ebb9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373804} {:answer/id #uuid "708d1932-73c6-5f7d-ae3b-5e1d6d9045b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bbf909f-462f-5518-8013-a606f7cf41aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373803} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ed6ed5-0a52-5e29-8c5e-f028a3e0baf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "deb34ff2-c563-5932-95e2-cff733776593" :question/math-academy-id "q-143125" :question/problem "The volume of a cube increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the length of its edge. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the edge length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cube **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **length of its edge**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cube* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *inversely proportional to the length of its edge.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cube is given by\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{s} \\\\ 3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{3{s}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{3}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{3}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d868c89-ca9e-589d-917e-cfec472a44f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Two-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0769ab1a-87bc-5eff-8aeb-99c8fc7755ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500480} {:answer/id #uuid "23680e06-ba93-52a3-b975-55fcee12e521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K{r}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500540} {:answer/id #uuid "44e2023a-8e7d-5888-a40a-68baea3726dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{r}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500603} {:answer/id #uuid "8f157fdf-0dbd-517d-b358-049a5969dec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500765} {:answer/id #uuid "e597013b-e59c-54da-9a35-6be52c89dceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44e2023a-8e7d-5888-a40a-68baea3726dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{r}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500603} :answer-field/id #uuid "5708293f-0ffb-5c47-a00d-e170d13fb197" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "842c8f8f-382b-5ffe-9458-677d53445332" :question/math-academy-id "q-143130" :question/problem "The area of a circle increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of its circumference. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **increases** at a **rate** that's **inversely proportional** to **the square of its circumference**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *inversely proportional* to *the square of its circumference.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{(2\\pi r)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{(2\\pi r)^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{4{\\pi }^{2}{r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{8{\\pi }^{3}{r}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{8{\\pi }^{3}}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{r}^{3}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d868c89-ca9e-589d-917e-cfec472a44f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Two-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44aa8350-415a-5f9c-b0de-81bc08e238c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373822} {:answer/id #uuid "f1641817-2c87-59b4-b4f0-67eb29c03041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373823} {:answer/id #uuid "f685a9e1-aaf6-5e7b-a9e6-a1f060c8d1ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373824} {:answer/id #uuid "b877dcdf-d8f4-5a0a-ad47-fe6c14197fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373825} {:answer/id #uuid "d94f34bd-600c-5304-a9c0-6d081c68ab35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1641817-2c87-59b4-b4f0-67eb29c03041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373823} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d00b91-6897-5427-9c98-70d18a8a25c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdeb725d-e8ab-53b6-a397-2087096b75de" :question/math-academy-id "q-143131" :question/problem "The area of a circle decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **decreases** at a **rate** that's **inversely proportional** to its **radius**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *inversely proportional* to its *radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{r}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{r} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k}{2\\pi {r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d868c89-ca9e-589d-917e-cfec472a44f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Two-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b33e11a-e522-5400-8bb3-afd63970b187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500490} {:answer/id #uuid "53318ba9-1b15-543d-9d80-95909854731c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500638} {:answer/id #uuid "85fe4b15-915f-5ca8-a203-8af14de723fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500746} {:answer/id #uuid "b11d0e7a-c89d-5592-b063-2bcfc835c8a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=Ks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500836} {:answer/id #uuid "cca7a708-1831-5d8d-95ae-358f0a1f9723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53318ba9-1b15-543d-9d80-95909854731c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500638} :answer-field/id #uuid "e953d84f-9d70-56d1-a2e6-25501f223dc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8a543fb6-3a0e-5397-9156-31fc05eeae07" :question/math-academy-id "q-143134" :question/problem "The area of a square increases at a rate proportional to the cube of its perimeter. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a square **increases** at a rate **proportional** to the **cube of its perimeter**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a square* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate *proportional to the cube of its perimeter*. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k(4s)^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a square is given by \n\n$$\nA={s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =k(4s)^{3} \\\\ 2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =64k{s}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{64k{s}^{3}}{2s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =32k{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=32k>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188257 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d868c89-ca9e-589d-917e-cfec472a44f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Two-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0fb4ff0-8aba-56c6-bf18-44b6befa2526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-K{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373843} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0b3c0e-b662-5862-a4c5-679402ce0bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=K{A}^{5/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373844} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3d4487-e746-5e3c-9c6e-dc0aa305a8aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-K{A}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373845} {:answer/id #uuid "c4368a2d-1df2-525b-96d3-28c62a09439d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373846} {:answer/id #uuid "c54d10a3-7468-590f-b69a-d71e43ed2923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=KA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd3d4487-e746-5e3c-9c6e-dc0aa305a8aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-K{A}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373845} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7d7a9b-3a3b-5028-ad76-6fd5d5b5cf8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "20695e23-caba-5b56-9712-3007924a9859" :question/math-academy-id "q-143136" :question/problem "The side length of a square decreases at a rate proportional to its area. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the area, $A(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **side length** of a square **decreases** at a rate **proportional** to its **area**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *side length $s$ of a square* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate *proportional to the area of the square*. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-kA\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe side length of a square is given by \n\n$$\ns=\\sqrt{A}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{A}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{A}}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-kA \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{A}}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-kA \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2k{A}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=2k>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-K{A}^{3/2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "474f9c82-4ae7-58e0-9457-6bda9cab8973" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Coordinate Plane Problems Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9494b15f-f120-5e41-bde4-5fecd3535616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=2|y|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500773} {:answer/id #uuid "adef745d-e631-557e-874a-c44d15d825e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=4|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500827} {:answer/id #uuid "b8426d7e-d065-50db-bf82-777624091a2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{|y|}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500850} {:answer/id #uuid "c1525df8-367a-5d9b-b7b9-3446e2adefb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{|y|}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500869} {:answer/id #uuid "cd022d2f-f204-5579-bb94-25cfa4ed54b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{|x|}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9494b15f-f120-5e41-bde4-5fecd3535616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=2|y|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500773} :answer-field/id #uuid "917748b4-b224-5d7a-96ef-e578265796e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9801499d-e8ba-50b1-a83f-440b9081ac28" :question/math-academy-id "q-143142" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis. The derivative at the point $(-3,6)$ equals $12.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis* is given by $|y|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot |y|\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k|y|.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=12$ when at the point $(-3,6).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12=k|6|\\,⟹\\,k=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=2|y|.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "474f9c82-4ae7-58e0-9457-6bda9cab8973" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Coordinate Plane Problems Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59b992c6-a6c5-5a2a-bea8-00115a148a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{12y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500651} {:answer/id #uuid "89ee39bb-f1f9-5ca9-937e-5d500320a292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500757} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ff4381-b2a5-5d9c-a6aa-03d02c2f7df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500797} {:answer/id #uuid "c4209c74-841c-5648-8653-0db964c34b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500878} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d8375a-8649-5db7-9cc9-00f70e9c364c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{6y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89ee39bb-f1f9-5ca9-937e-5d500320a292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500757} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fe16f66-cbec-5dab-913b-319990c8cc5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb3732e8-5921-5cbd-a1fe-55832a8ffa5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-144820" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin. The derivative at the point $(2,-4)$ equals $12.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *slope of the line through $P(x,y)$ and the origin* is given by $\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot {(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{k{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=12$ when at the point $(2,-4).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12=\\frac{k(-4)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{12}{4}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9609247-0e03-57ea-b5ae-e4d8317f21cd" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Contextual Problems Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98acc335-5a05-57d1-8371-293cd7062145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-kM+1\\,500,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375084} {:answer/id #uuid "2d341451-5f26-50af-969e-8ff3ed2e215f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-1,500M,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375085} {:answer/id #uuid "60aa858d-9503-5d2a-9bd7-87bbe3adcc86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=kM,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375086} {:answer/id #uuid "3884d47f-4d7a-559f-8e29-a3f62ce9ccb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=-kM-1\\,500,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375087} {:answer/id #uuid "8e937f25-0f1d-5bb9-9104-b1bda4614605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=kM-1\\,500,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e937f25-0f1d-5bb9-9104-b1bda4614605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=kM-1\\,500,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375088} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a47f3ba-08fc-5768-9a1b-fcb06056cdd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "390bc00c-f6b7-55a7-918a-e927d972b878" :question/math-academy-id "q-38250" :question/problem "The amount of money $M(t)$ in a saving account increases at a rate that's proportional to the amount currently in the account. The account holder takes $1\\,500$ dollars out of the account each year for emergencies. What is the differential equation that describes the rate of change in the amount of money in the account at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time (in years) since they opened the account?" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change in the number of dollars in the account per year increases in proportion to $M$ and decreases by $1\\,500.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=kM-1\\,500,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a (positive) constant of proportionality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba211dba-23ad-5755-b1e3-082e13be59a2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Three-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021cf654-7170-5d38-a79a-42fe86f478ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-kr+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500472} {:answer/id #uuid "0bcce3e0-856f-50b2-a996-ed820481fba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\pi k{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500493} {:answer/id #uuid "14c405ae-7793-5579-b31f-2de4fa47beb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500513} {:answer/id #uuid "638194a1-7794-5512-a783-f3c9c287538f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500674} {:answer/id #uuid "8793e775-b1b8-51bf-8191-d8fb79ae27e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-3kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "638194a1-7794-5512-a783-f3c9c287538f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500674} :answer-field/id #uuid "73b83985-1ed6-5dc2-90bb-7281bf26a7e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "216b8c3f-43b1-55fb-bffc-27ac09c14aab" :question/math-academy-id "q-38252" :question/problem "Tom's spherical basketball has a hole in it. Its volume decreases at a rate that's proportional to its surface area. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ of the ball. Assume that $k>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*Its **volume decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **surface area**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its surface area $S.$* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kS,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$ Now, using the fact that $S=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\pi k{r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{2}}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188913 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9609247-0e03-57ea-b5ae-e4d8317f21cd" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Contextual Problems Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca0e9268-1caf-523c-b9f2-33b5e344e302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-kA+2,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402630} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b05b1a-5684-5d7b-b75e-a7617a17e248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\sqrt{kA}-2,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402631} {:answer/id #uuid "86f4b722-93dd-52c5-a40c-7b44b017ce4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA-2,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402632} {:answer/id #uuid "5356c73e-9cfd-516a-8bfe-6eeb1eccf437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA+2,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402633} {:answer/id #uuid "f351aa2b-4c9b-5a80-930e-82035730d517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86f4b722-93dd-52c5-a40c-7b44b017ce4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA-2,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402632} :answer-field/id #uuid "79fbfb09-aa66-5f0f-8220-7411bb5f5920" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2aeb7023-942d-59e4-9752-79902203013d" :question/math-academy-id "q-38324" :question/problem "The size of a bush growing in a schoolyard is being monitored. The area $A\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}$ of the bush at any given moment grows at a rate that's directly proportional to the area at that moment. Additionally, $2\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}$ of the bush is cut away every month.\nWhat is the differential equation that describes the rate of change in the area of the schoolyard bush at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time in months since it started growing?" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change in the area of the bush per month increases in proportion to $A$ and decreases by $2.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the (positive) constant of proportionality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba211dba-23ad-5755-b1e3-082e13be59a2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Three-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ad34be-911c-538b-9802-3b400981630c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500535} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7d0bb7-352a-5bf9-a7f5-302351f7d4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500549} {:answer/id #uuid "37965923-72fe-55e3-94ae-308e2569746c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500579} {:answer/id #uuid "46633668-b0a3-53ef-86c3-cafb733f4e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500606} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b2d8d2-d36b-5317-a242-1e5495b2e405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20ad34be-911c-538b-9802-3b400981630c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500535} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc9d2d16-1277-5174-8bfa-2ed096aba409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e12d746-15a9-5bf9-9bc2-9113fc7fbbef" :question/math-academy-id "q-38598" :question/problem "The volume of a sphere increases at a rate proportional to the square of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a sphere **increases** at a rate **proportional** to the **square of its radius**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate *proportional to the square of its radius.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =k{r}^{2} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{4\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{4\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba211dba-23ad-5755-b1e3-082e13be59a2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Three-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61117aac-0ddd-5b18-90b2-d250afca608b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500666} {:answer/id #uuid "70e8b4fb-f4e5-5b00-9546-7908b751149c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500699} {:answer/id #uuid "c817eb95-9eb3-52b3-8f8b-7dc081fb2ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500891} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc3c7fc-fe9f-5c7b-8cbd-d47330b88535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500941} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ff4ab0-084f-5bf6-8cc1-99171d95158a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70e8b4fb-f4e5-5b00-9546-7908b751149c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500699} :answer-field/id #uuid "e801f5f0-f458-5f7b-91b1-d27a8bacf367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8090d53-1681-5d0f-a69a-1950471f0a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-66523" :question/problem "Tom's basketball has a hole in it, meaning its volume decreases at a rate that's proportional to its volume. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ of a ball. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of the ball **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **volume**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its volume.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-kV \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{(\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3})}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{12\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{kr}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{3}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9609247-0e03-57ea-b5ae-e4d8317f21cd" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Contextual Problems Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fc830cc-ece8-5496-97a7-70e65565df26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV+10,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500499} {:answer/id #uuid "105b83e4-246b-55e2-b4ab-738341a8abea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=V-10k,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500503} {:answer/id #uuid "754197e2-db12-5135-a97c-80f3df146721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV-10,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500708} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdf12f4-4e3f-5902-b339-3b9f7049068e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV+10,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500723} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3f9812-08b3-55fc-a348-c9861184305c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-V-10,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fc830cc-ece8-5496-97a7-70e65565df26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV+10,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500499} :answer-field/id #uuid "00c35029-688f-585b-b372-8febae786a22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1800d774-9d7c-5ba7-a1e5-da93acbae2ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-79310" :question/problem "A cyclist has a small puncture in one of her tires. The volume of air $V,$ in centimeters cubed, in the tire decreases at a rate that's proportional to the volume at that moment. Every day, the cyclist pumps $10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ back into the tire.\nWhat is the differential equation describing the rate of change of the volume of air in the tire  at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time in days?" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change in the volume of air per day decreases in proportion to $V$ and increases by $12.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV+10,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the (positive) constant of proportionality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba211dba-23ad-5755-b1e3-082e13be59a2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Three-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fce5104-1668-5712-b8bd-4600d8fe3f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500500} {:answer/id #uuid "5e91d50d-2e23-50af-b2c1-3fb740beeb3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-K{s}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500663} {:answer/id #uuid "74861129-d701-503d-80d2-8fd2c0956239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500705} {:answer/id #uuid "e573b28e-6822-53dc-896b-d87246576b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500955} {:answer/id #uuid "e7efebb7-1731-5b5b-bba6-ee401ac97130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-Ks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e573b28e-6822-53dc-896b-d87246576b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500955} :answer-field/id #uuid "23a5fc86-bde8-5b42-aec1-bd6dc60c978a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "87dafc6b-b002-5f20-a4d1-b738e1cc353e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80491" :question/problem "The volume of a cube decreases at a rate that's proportional to its surface area. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cube **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **surface area**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cube* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its surface area $S.$* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kS,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$ Now, using the fact that $S=6{s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-6k{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cube is given by\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-6k{s}^{2} \\\\ 3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-6k{s}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{6k{s}^{2}}{3{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=2k>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-K.\n$$"}]}
